		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="025da6f4-b1b6-423a-8c0f-b324f531a6f1"
			id="1.5 Plant"
			parent="1 Universe, creation">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for general words for all plants. Use a book of pictures to identify plant names and the scientific name. Languages divide plants into various domains that are not always comparable from language to language. Criterial features may be characteristics (trees and bushes are distinguished by size and number of trunks) and use (grass and weeds are distinguished by their desirability). A common distinction is between trees and non-trees, with trees described as being big, woody, and having a life expectancy of several years, while non-trees are small, non-woody, and have a life expectancy of typically not more than one year (Heine, Bernd and Karsten Legere. 1995. Swahili plants. Rudiger Koppe Verlag: Koln.). Agricultural societies will divide plants into wild and cultivated. However most plants for which there are names have some use. Therefore it does not seem helpful to divide plant names into domains for useful and non-useful plants. Since only parts of plants are eaten, edible parts of plants are listed under the domain 'Food'. Some languages may have more domains than are used in this list, others may have fewer. The classification system used here does not agree entirely with the system used by botanists. For instance botanists do not classify all the tree species together. The palm trees belong to the class Monocotyledoneae and are classified with lilies, bananas, and orchids. Apple and cherry trees belong to the class Dicotyledoneae and are classified in the rose family along with roses and blackberries. The acacia tree also belongs to the class Dicotyledoneae and is classified in the pulse family along with lupines and beans. However most folk taxonomies bring all the trees together. The scientific classification system for plants and animals is taken from: Carruth, Gorton, ed. 1989. The Volume Library, Vols. 1 and 2. The Southwestern Company: Nashville.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Plant</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="0ee5b933-f1ab-485f-894a-51fe239cb726"
			id="1.5.1 Tree"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for trees--flowering plants with roots, stems, and leaves, which are large and have a wooden trunk (Phylum Spermatophyta, Subdivision Angiospermae). Also include the evergreen trees (Phylum Spermatophyta, Subdivision Gymnospermae). Evergreen trees do not have flowers, but have cone like fruits (pinecones) that contain seeds. Their leaves are shaped like needles and are retained for over a year.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Tree</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.1</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="531af868-b5fb-41c2-ba50-764458f9102f"
			id="1.5.2 Bush, shrub"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for bushes and shrubs--plants that are smaller than trees and have several wooden trunks (Phylum Spermatophyta, Subdivision Angiospermae).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Bush, shrub</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.2</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="c345f278-91ff-463d-b9a6-8abac8a267eb"
			id="1.5.3 Grass, herb, vine"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for small plants that have roots, stems, flowers, and seeds, but do not have a wooden trunk (Phylum Spermatophyta, Subdivision Angiospermae). Also include the seedless plants, such as ferns (phylum Pteridophyta, class Filicineae), horsetails (phylum Pteridophyta, class Equisetineae), and club mosses (phylum Pteridophyta, class Lycopodineae). Plants of the Pteridophyta phylum have no flowers or seeds. Ferns have large, divided, feather-like leaves, or fronds. Club mosses are small (rarely over one meter) evergreen plants with simple leaves resembling pine or hemlock needles. Sometimes they grow upright, but often trail on the ground, where they propagate by means of runners. Horsetails send up tall, vertical, jointed stalks with branches covered with scale like leaves.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Grass, herb, vine</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.3</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="d06dae77-134a-403f-ba88-52ecd66c0522"
			id="1.5.4 Moss, fungus, algae"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for mosses (phylum Bryophyta, class Musci), liverworts (phylum Bryophyta, class Hepaticae), fungi (phylum Thallophyta, subdivision Fungi), algae (phylum Thallophyta, subdivision Algae), and lichens. These plants do not have true roots, stems, or leaves. The mosses are small, green, flowerless plants that grow in moist environments and look like velvety or feathery growths carpeting the ground, tree trunks, and rocks. The liverworts are similar to the mosses with a flat and branching growth pattern. The algae possess green chlorophyll. They vary from one-celled organisms, which sometimes live in colonies such as pond scum, to complex organisms such as seaweed. The fungi do not possess chlorophyll and feed off of other organic material. Lichens consist of a fungus and an algae growing together. They commonly grow on trunks of trees and rocks. Some are flat and leafy, and some are moss like.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Moss, fungus, algae</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.4</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="d117aa22-3f18-47c4-9683-51ecf1dc7134"
			id="1.5.5 Parts of a plant"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for words that refer to parts of a plant. Start with general words that all plants have. Then think through each major type of plant. Finish by thinking of specific plants that are well known (usually cultivated crops) and that have words for specific parts (e.g. tassel on a corn/maize cob).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Parts of a plant</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.5</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="d2ca3194-e393-480e-ae1d-dd67bed55227"
			id="1.5.6 Growth of plants"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for words related to the growth of plants.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Growth of plants</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.6</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="f7b7eb3c-b784-4ba5-8dac-a68fd27ce0ea"
			id="1.5.7 Plant diseases"
			parent="1.5 Plant">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for words related to plant diseases.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Plant diseases</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.5.7</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="944cf5af-469e-4b03-878f-a05d34b0d9f6"
			id="1.6 Animal"
			parent="1 Universe, creation">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for general words referring to animals.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Animal</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="73499b8b-76fc-4121-8bfa-1bdebe537259"
			id="1.6.1 Types of animals"
			parent="1.6 Animal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for words describing types of animals. Use a book of pictures to identify each species and its scientific name. This section is organized according to the scientific, biological classification of animals. It may not correspond to the local classification of animals (folk taxonomy), which are often based on how people relate to animals (tame/wild, edible/work). Use this domain for words referring to large classes of animals that do not correspond to the scientific classification. For instance this would be the place for a word like 'flying animal', which includes birds, bats, and flying insects.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Types of animals</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="5e9f361d-17dc-4ada-a312-8da269d22a64"
			id="1.6.1.1 Mammal"
			parent="1.6.1 Types of animals">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for general words referring to mammals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Mammal</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="40248a12-1809-4561-b786-e4e274c14d82"
			id="1.6.1.1.1 Primate"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for primates (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Primates).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Primate</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.1</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="56ef3f06-7fb9-462e-a7d0-517f3ce1623f"
			id="1.6.1.1.2 Carnivore"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for carnivores--meat-eating animals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Carnivora).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Carnivore</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.2</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="bbd3c3f1-7387-4ec6-a75d-66c1355a94ef"
			id="1.6.1.1.3 Hoofed animals"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for even-toed hoofed animals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Artiodactyla), odd-toed hoofed animals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Perissodactyla), and elephants (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Proboscidea).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Hoofed animals</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.3</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="a8b9e892-df3e-44c4-8c68-7a0f7f4468bb"
			id="1.6.1.1.4 Rodent"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for rodents--gnawing animals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Rodentia), insect eating animals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Insectivora), rabbits (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Lagomorpha), and hyraxes (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Hyracoidea).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Rodent</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.4</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="31171aa9-e243-4b46-abd8-f3e52843cdfc"
			id="1.6.1.1.5 Marsupial"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for marsupials (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Marsupialia). Marsupials carry their young in a pouch.</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Marsupial</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.5</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="445f3084-f250-40fa-87ba-ebd233f9018f"
			id="1.6.1.1.6 Anteater, aardvark"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for mammals with few or no teeth--anteaters (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Edentata), pangolins (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Pholidota), aardvarks (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Tubulidentata), and platypus--mammals that lay eggs (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Monotremata).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Anteater, aardvark</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.6</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
		<range-element
			guid="e22d860a-d207-4649-8ab5-4592b838febb"
			id="1.6.1.1.7 Sea mammal"
			parent="1.6.1.1 Mammal">
			<description>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Use this domain for mammals that live in the sea--whales and dolphins (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Cetacea), seals (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Pinnipedia), and sea cows (phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Sirenia).</text>
				</form>
			</description>
			<label>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>Sea mammal</text>
				</form>
			</label>
			<abbrev>
				<form
					lang="en">
					<text>1.6.1.1.7</text>
				</form>
			</abbrev>
		</range-element>
